---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CardDetail from '../components/CardDetail.astro';
import FamilyLine from '../components/FamilyLine.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import FeatureList from '../components/FeatureList.astro';
import { CURRENT, EDGE_FEATURES, STATUS, product } from '../lib/products';

const p = product('edge');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Edge: store-and-forward and an OPC UA server"
  description="HotLoop Edge is IoT plus the machine layer: store-and-forward that survives a dead WAN, and an OPC UA server for your SCADA. 4.18.10 is out."
  ogAlt="HotLoop Edge, IoT plus the machine layer."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: p.name,
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/edge/',
      description:
        'HotLoop IoT plus the machine layer: store-and-forward over Sparkplug B that survives a dead uplink, and an OPC UA server, for a box that sits next to a machine.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      softwareVersion: CURRENT,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop Edge</span><span class="chip ok">{CURRENT} is out</span></div>
      <h1>IoT, plus everything that lives next to a machine.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Ignition Edge had the right idea, and this is ours. Everything in <a href="/iot/">HotLoop IoT</a>, plus
        store-and-forward to whatever sits upstream and an OPC UA server. The upshot: the machine keeps running its
        screens and its logic when the network doesn't, and the SCADA you already paid for can still read it.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>HotLoop Edge {CURRENT} is out. It's the first release of Edge, and it ships beside IoT, the Gateway, and the Edge Relay from the same tag.</strong>
          <p>
            The image is <code>{p.image}:{CURRENT}</code> for amd64, arm64 and arm/v7, about 34 MB on arm, and it pulls with no
            login. The chart is <code>{p.chart}</code> from <code>https://hotloop.io/hotloop</code>. If the box only has
            to poll and forward, the <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a> is the smaller one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install it</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/products/">Compare the lineup</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://docs.hotloop.io/edge/overview/">Docs</a>
      </div>
      <FamilyLine id="edge" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">What it is for</span>
        <h2>A box at the machine that keeps working when the network doesn't.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          The operator's screen runs against the box on the machine, not a server three buildings away. When the
          uplink drops, readings buffer and replay in order, flagged historical, when it comes back. The
          alarm still raises, the rule still runs, the panel still answers. A backhoe through the fiber is an IT
          ticket, not a line down. A hard kill with the link up loses what was in memory, about one poll, and the
          docs say so.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">The database</span>
        <h2>{p.databases}.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Picked by a setting, in the same image. TimescaleDB when the box keeps real history, SQLite when it's a small PC
          with a small disk. Moving up to the Gateway is designed to keep your data and your config, so the box you
          start with isn't a dead end.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What Edge adds to IoT</span>
        <h2>What Edge adds, and where each piece stands.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          "{STATUS.released.label}" means it ships in the published release. "{STATUS.unverified.label}" means the
          path exists and nobody has pointed it at the real thing yet, so your first one is a test, not an install.
          "{STATUS.planned.label}" means exactly that. Everything in <a href="/iot/">IoT</a> comes along too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <FeatureList features={EDGE_FEATURES} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">HMI, what is built and what is the plan</span>
        <h2>One UI, not two.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          HMI screens aren't a second front end bolted on the side. They're the dashboards IoT gets, bound to a
          physical screen, and that part is out in every product with a UI. The symbol library and faceplates below are the plan, the Edge HMI work that is still ahead. They use the same
          bindings and the same write gate. Two UIs means two sets of bugs and two places to
          forget a permission check, and we're not signing up for that.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>A canvas that never reflows</h3><p>A fixed screen that scales and never shuffles things around, because an operator's thumb goes to where the pump was yesterday, and it had better still be there.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>ISA-101 by default</h3><p>Gray backgrounds, color only for abnormal, alarm priority by shape as well as color. When the screen is gray, the one red thing gets seen.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Faceplates</h3><p>Click a motor and get its popup: start, stop, reset, mode, speed, its interlocks, and its alarms, built from the motor entity that already exists.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Panel mode</h3><p>Open the box at <code>/?screen=name</code> and the dashboard bound to that name fills the panel: no header, big touch targets, and it works with the uplink down.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>A person behind every write</h3><p>On your own computer you're already signed in. A shared panel signs operators in with a PIN or a badge, so the audit names a human, not "panel 3".</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>What is not built</h3><p>The ISA-101 symbol library, the faceplates and the PIN sign-in for shared panels are design. Dashboards, which they build on, are in {CURRENT}, and this page will say when these move.</p></article>
      </div>
      <CardDetail>
        <div><h4>ISA-101 by default</h4><p>Gray backgrounds, color only for abnormal, alarm priority by shape as well as color. When the whole screen is gray, the one red thing gets seen. No 3D gradients on the tank.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Panel mode</h4><p>Open the box at <code>/?screen=name</code> and the dashboard bound to that name fills the panel with no header, no tabs and big touch targets. It works against the box itself with the uplink down. Panel mode drops the header, so the alarm list on a panel is an alarms card on its dashboard.</p></div>
        <div><h4>A person behind every write</h4><p>On your own computer you're already signed in. A shared panel signs operators in with a PIN or a badge and signs them out when idle, so the audit names a human, not "panel 3".</p></div>
      </CardDetail>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Install</span>
        <h2>Helm on a cluster, Quadlet on a box.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          One chart repository holds all four charts. Call the release <code>hotloop</code> whichever product it is, so
          the box can move up to the Gateway later without renaming a volume.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Helm</h3>
<pre><code>helm repo add hotloop https://hotloop.io/hotloop
helm repo update
helm install hotloop hotloop/hotloop-edge --version {CURRENT} \
  --namespace hotloop --create-namespace</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            The chart runs TimescaleDB by default and keeps 90 days of history. <code>database.type</code> also takes
            {' '}<code>postgresql</code> and <code>sqlite</code>. Store-and-forward is the Sparkplug B edge publishing
            block in the chart values, and its queue is capped at 64 MiB by default.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Quadlet</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            For a box outside any cluster. The Edge units are under <code>deploy/quadlet/edge/</code> in the
            {' '}<code>hotloop</code> repository, with <code>deploy/quadlet/edge/sqlite/</code> for a database that is a
            file. That repository is private for now, so the
            {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/install/quadlet/">Quadlet install guide</a> is where to read how they
            work. They need Podman 5.0 or newer.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            The app follows <code>:stable</code> and rolls back a release that can't start.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Not proven yet</span>
        <h2>What we haven't run, so you don't find out at commissioning.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The Quadlet units under systemd</h3><p>The Gateway's units were run rootless under systemd. Edge's were generated from them and checked, not started. A real Pi under systemd as root: not yet.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The 32-bit arm image</h3><p>The arm/v7 image is built and published. It hasn't met a Pi 3 under load yet.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Moving up a product on a cluster</h3><p>IoT to Edge to the Gateway on one database volume has been run on Quadlet. As a <code>helm upgrade</code> on a real cluster, and going back down, it has not.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="notice after">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>S7comm and EtherNet/IP are decode tested and have never touched a physical PLC.</strong>
          <p>
            The first real controller you point either one at is commissioning, not a formality. Check a value you
            already know before you trust a screen. <a href="/integrations/">The integrations page</a> says the same.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <CardDetail>
        <div><h4>The Quadlet units under systemd</h4><p>The Gateway's units were run rootless under systemd, including an update that can't start and gets rolled back. Edge's were generated from them and checked against them, and not started. A real Pi under systemd as root: not yet.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Moving up a product on a cluster</h4><p>IoT to Edge to the Gateway on one database volume has been run on Quadlet. The same move as a <code>helm upgrade</code> on a real cluster, and going back down, has not.</p></div>
      </CardDetail>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free, with no license key.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            Edge is under the HotLoop Business Source License 1.1, like the rest of the lineup. Home and lab use is
            free. Business use is free through <a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNet</a>. Edge isn't a paid tier of
            anything, because there is no paid tier to be one of.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the license</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .after { margin-top: var(--hl-s6); }
  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
